    "turn_text": "Um, so, um, got a phone call and I won this bizarre one vote, um, committee vote because the guy before me got out because of alcoholism, which is ironic. I own a distillery, but that's probably another show. But so, and who I beat was somebody who was so far right and really considered themselves a dominionist. Um, that I came in as a guy who even when they asked me, are you a evangelical Christian? I'm like, it's none of your business, but I'm not. I mean, you know, and I'm saying things that I know makes Republican consultants squirm, right? I believe something looked out for me a few times and places that were awful, but I'm just not that, right? And I think part of this too, I was raised LDS, right? And, you know, I'm not really a practicing anything. I was raised LDS and my grandparents were Southern Baptist. you know, had a bizarre upbringing. And, you know, so I thought, well, goodness, I won by one vote, so I must have a mandate. So, I'm going to do exactly what I think I should do as the right thing. So, I'm taking votes, I'm doing my thing. And then, and Alex and rec, they said, Denver, would you marry us? I'm like, sure. I honestly, it wasn't like, I'm like, oh, my political future. What do I do here? If I was thinking about my political future, I would never marry them in that specific district. But I married them in an incredible ceremony. So, I officiated a same-sex wedding. The issue that I had wasn't just the same sex wedding though, when I went to Southside, the fact is it was an interracial same sex wedding. Um, I think that's the thing that really surprised me was that, um, and also as you know, I have a stance on marijuana that doesn't really align, uh, with Republicans, by the way I'm growing hemp right now. So I think those things",
